# Files chezmoi should delete from the target on apply.
#
# The per-distro bashrc variants were merged into ~/.config/shell/ (they differed
# by exactly one alias). Listing them here removes the stale copies from every
# machine, not just the one the merge was done on.
.bashrc-debian
.bashrc-arch

# alacritty is no longer used. Deleting home/dot_config/alacritty/ from the
# source stops it being applied to NEW machines, but leaves the already-applied
# copies orphaned in ~ on every machine that ran an earlier apply — chezmoi does
# not remove a target just because its source vanished. Listing them here does.
.config/alacritty/alacritty.toml
.config/alacritty/catppuccin-mocha.toml
.config/alacritty

# Dormant kitty variants. NOTHING selected them — no host-switching logic ever
# referenced either file, so they were reference copies that drifted (um690 was
# a near-duplicate of kitty.conf's 2000-line default dump, and still carried the
# stale jetbrains font_family). Same orphan rule as alacritty above: deleting the
# source alone would leave them applied in ~ forever.
.config/kitty/kitty.typecraft.conf
.config/kitty/kitty.um690.conf

# The Future Tech Holdings Teleport login helper. That site is no longer
# operational, so the script logs into a cluster that does not exist. Same orphan
# rule as alacritty above — and it matters more here, because the applied copy is
# an EXECUTABLE on PATH: left behind, `tsh-login-fth.sh` stays tab-completable and
# still shells out to `op item get "FTH Teleport"` against a dead proxy.
#
# Its sibling home/dot_local/bin/executable_tsh-login-syketech.sh is NOT retired —
# that cluster is live. See the tsh-login-op-hardening task.
.local/bin/tsh-login-fth.sh

# protoc, retired. It was pulled from the doctor registry and the installer in
# iter 27, but that only stopped NEW machines getting it: the fetcher script, the
# PATH symlink and the fetched payload were already applied, and chezmoi does not
# remove a target just because its source vanished. Every apply since has left
# this box with a protoc on PATH that nothing in the repo knew about, no
# fetcher would ever update, and the uninstall path could not remove — because
# the installer entry that would have removed it was deleted along with the rest.
#
# All three surfaces are listed. The payload glob is deliberate: other machines
# are on other versions, and a version-pinned entry would miss them.
#
# If protoc is ever wanted again it comes back as a proper fetcher slot with a
# platform-table entry and a teardown case — and these three lines come out.
.local/bin/fetch.bins/12_fetch.protoc.sh
.local/bin/protoc
.local/apps/protoc-*
